WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.323
Welcome to Module 2, Programming and
Downloading. We'll do two

00:00:04.323 --> 00:00:05.160
things here.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.925
First, program and initialize the device
correctly. Then, once

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:10.200
the wear period ends,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.985
get the data off it cleanly and
completely. Before you program

00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:15.680
a device,

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.801
run through this pre-initialization
checklist. Study and

00:00:18.801 --> 00:00:19.860
device IDs, planned

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.004
wear location, start and stop schedule,
sampling settings,

00:00:23.004 --> 00:00:23.600
participant

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.769
instructions, and the operator responsible
for programming.

00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:27.360
Confirm all

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.054
six before you start. Also, document the
final valid-day rule

00:00:31.054 --> 00:00:31.720
before data

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.358
collection begins. It is a later quality
control criterion,

00:00:35.358 --> 00:00:36.160
not a setting

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.593
programmed into the device. In this course
example, a valid

00:00:39.593 --> 00:00:40.640
participant record

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.414
requires at least four valid days, at
least 10 hours of awake

00:00:44.414 --> 00:00:45.280
wear per valid

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.890
day, and at least one weekend day. Set
your own criteria from

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:49.600
the approved

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.480
protocol. Every deployment starts right
here. Seat the device

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.000
in the USB dock and

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.669
connect it to the computer. Here's the
full click path for

00:01:00.669 --> 00:01:02.360
initializing. Connect

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.389
the device, click Initialize, and choose
Regular

00:01:05.389 --> 00:01:06.840
Initialization. Set the

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.719
protocol-approved start and stop
schedule—seven days in this

00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:12.400
course example. Open Enter

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.343
Subject Info and use the study-approved
coded participant ID

00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:17.000
and device

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.900
assignment. Use clear only if the approved
protocol requires

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:21.680
the existing

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.659
subject information to be reset. Then
confirm the device and

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:26.720
click initialize

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.440
one device. Let's walk through it live.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.180
With the device connected, click
initialize in ActiLife and

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:34.560
choose

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.722
regular initialization to open the
initialization parameters.

00:01:38.722 --> 00:01:39.200
Set the

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.670
protocol-approved collection start and
stop times—seven days

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:43.480
in this course

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.619
example. The sample rate, LED, and
recording options must also

00:01:47.619 --> 00:01:48.220
match the

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.853
approved protocol. The values on screen
are examples, so do

00:01:51.853 --> 00:01:52.900
not reuse another

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.999
cohort settings. When the settings are
correct, click enter

00:01:56.999 --> 00:01:58.180
subject info. Use

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.030
clear only if the approved protocol
requires the existing

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:01.380
subject

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.120
information to be reset. Then enter the
study-approved coded

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.180
participant ID in

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.868
the subject name field. The demo uses a
placeholder. Do not

00:02:09.868 --> 00:02:11.180
enter a participant's

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.427
personal name unless the approved protocol
explicitly requires

00:02:15.427 --> 00:02:16.180
it. Confirm

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:19.120
that the coded ID and device serial number
match the

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.420
deployment log exactly.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:24.140
Finally, select the device and click
initialize one device. It

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:24.500
is now

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.820
programmed and ready for deployment.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:31.597
The device goes out and the participant
wears it for seven

00:02:31.597 --> 00:02:32.900
straight days. We'll

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.375
fast-forward. Seven days later, the device
comes back and we

00:02:36.375 --> 00:02:36.780
move to

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:41.641
downloading. Download stage one is five
steps. Open ActiLife

00:02:41.641 --> 00:02:43.180
and click download.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:46.674
Use change location to select the
study-approved raw data

00:02:46.674 --> 00:02:47.900
folder. Confirm each

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.986
device's coded participant record. Keep
the epoch at 10

00:02:50.986 --> 00:02:52.220
seconds for this video

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:57.320
workflow and click download all devices.
Let's see it in

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.140
ActiLife.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.467
Once the device is back, connect the
return devices, click

00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:02.500
download, and open

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.585
the download options. Use Change Location
to select the

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:07.380
study-approved access-controlled

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.673
raw data folder and keep that path
consistent across the

00:03:10.673 --> 00:03:12.260
batch. On the right, select

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.801
right, select create AGD file and keep the
10-second epoch

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:16.900
used in this video

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:20.955
workflow. Use a de-identified filename
such as study ID,

00:03:20.955 --> 00:03:22.620
device ID, and download

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.017
date. Never include a participant's name.
Before downloading,

00:03:26.017 --> 00:03:27.020
confirm that every

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:30.324
expected device has a row and that the
coded participant ID

00:03:30.324 --> 00:03:30.940
matches the

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.608
deployment log. Click download all
devices. The demo device

00:03:34.608 --> 00:03:35.540
has no data and

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:39.384
is skipped. Click OK and continue with the
return devices. Do

00:03:39.384 --> 00:03:40.140
not unplug a

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.532
device until its status reads finished
downloading. ActiLife

00:03:43.532 --> 00:03:44.380
first downloads

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:48.641
the raw GT3X file and then creates the
10-second AGD summary.

00:03:48.641 --> 00:03:49.340
Update the

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.002
download log with the date, device ID,
coded participant ID,

00:03:53.002 --> 00:03:54.100
operator, approved

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.793
file location, and any return or device
issues. Finally,

00:03:57.793 --> 00:03:58.980
confirm that every

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.133
expected record produced a non-empty file.
Flag missing or

00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:03.220
suspicious files for

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:06.503
review, then route them using the
documented complete or

00:04:06.503 --> 00:04:07.500
incomplete triage

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:12.857
rule shown later. After downloading, each
record usually comes

00:04:12.857 --> 00:04:13.980
as two files.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.583
Don't mix them up. The GT3X is the raw
acceleration data and

00:04:18.583 --> 00:04:19.580
it's the main

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:23.993
input for GGIR in Module 4. The AGD is a
10-second epoch

00:04:23.993 --> 00:04:24.860
count-based

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:28.311
summary used for quick checks inside
ActiLife and if your

00:04:28.311 --> 00:04:29.340
pipeline needs it

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:34.460
you can also export CSV, a plain text
format.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:38.670
When the download is finished, open the
approved batch folder.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:39.660
Each record should

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.932
appear as a raw GT3X file and an AGD
summary. Open the AGD

00:04:43.932 --> 00:04:45.700
file in AGD File Viewer.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.235
Confirm that the epoch length is 10
seconds, then review the

00:04:49.235 --> 00:04:50.060
daily activity

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:53.418
graph. This is a preliminary check. First
confirm that data

00:04:53.418 --> 00:04:54.500
were recorded, then

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.443
compare the visible recording period with
the expected

00:04:57.443 --> 00:04:58.260
schedule. Final

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:02.835
participant validity is determined later
using the documented

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:04.260
valid-day criteria.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:08.917
This is stage 2. The visual inspection.
Compare each daily

00:05:08.917 --> 00:05:10.180
activity graph with

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:13.226
the planned collection period. The trace
on the left appears

00:05:13.226 --> 00:05:14.140
to cover all seven

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:18.157
days and may proceed to formal processing
and quality control.

00:05:18.157 --> 00:05:18.740
The trace

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.840
on the right contains data only in the
first few days and then

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:22.640
becomes blank so

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.316
it requires review. This visual check is
preliminary. It does

00:05:26.316 --> 00:05:27.100
not by itself

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:31.636
determine whether the participant meets
the final valid-day

00:05:31.636 --> 00:05:32.020
rule.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:36.134
This is an operational triage rule, not
the final valid-day

00:05:36.134 --> 00:05:37.420
rule. A record that

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:40.365
appears to contain at least half of the
planned collection

00:05:40.365 --> 00:05:41.380
period may enter the

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:45.938
complete folder for processing and quality
review. A record

00:05:45.938 --> 00:05:47.020
with less than

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:50.935
half, missing expected dates or a device
or download issue

00:05:50.935 --> 00:05:52.420
goes to incomplete for

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:56.363
review. In this seven day example, final
validity still

00:05:56.363 --> 00:05:57.940
requires at least four

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:01.918
valid days, at least 10 hours of awake
wear per valid-day and

00:06:01.918 --> 00:06:02.700
at least one

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:07.724
weekend day. Based on that preliminary
triage call, sort every

00:06:07.724 --> 00:06:08.940
record into two

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.088
folders. Records that appear to contain at
least half of the

00:06:12.088 --> 00:06:12.980
planned period go

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:16.592
into complete and proceed to processing
and formal quality

00:06:16.592 --> 00:06:17.900
control. Records with

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.620
less than half, missing dates or a
download or device issue go

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:21.860
into

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:25.283
incomplete for review. These folder labels
do not mean that a

00:06:25.283 --> 00:06:25.900
participant

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:29.740
has passed the final valid-day criteria.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:33.365
A quick recap. One, program the device
using the protocol

00:06:33.365 --> 00:06:34.700
approved schedule and

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:38.254
a coded participant ID. Two, download in
two stages, get the

00:06:38.254 --> 00:06:39.380
data off then check

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:43.358
it. Three, use the documented preliminary
triage rule to sort

00:06:43.358 --> 00:06:44.140
records into

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.776
complete versus incomplete. And four, take
notes at every

00:06:47.776 --> 00:06:49.180
step. Your log becomes

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.140
part of the QC record and audit trail.
